# CLIC: Product Cost & Infrastructure Cost Estimation (.NET Core & React.js)

This document outlines the detailed product development (CapEx) and operational infrastructure (OpEx) cost estimations for migrating the legacy **CLIC** (Climate/Crop Learning & Information Centre) CodeIgniter PHP application to a modern enterprise-grade stack: **.NET Core (ASP.NET Core Web API)** and **React.js**.

---

## 1. Application Architecture Overview

By migrating to a .NET Core and React.js stack, the application benefits from strong typing, high performance, cross-platform deployment, and robust enterprise design patterns (Dependency Injection, Repository Pattern, Entity Framework Core).

```mermaid
graph TD
    User([Farmer / Facilitator / Admin]) -->|HTTPS| ReactFront[React.js Frontend App]
    ReactFront -->|JSON / API Requests| DotNetAPI[ASP.NET Core Web API]
    DotNetAPI -->|Entity Framework Core| DbContext[Application DB Context]
    DbContext -->|Read/Write| RelationalDB[(PostgreSQL / MS SQL Server)]
    DotNetAPI -->|Background Service| HostedService[Background Tasks / Queue Workers]
    HostedService -->|Alert Triggers| MsgGateway[SMS / WhatsApp / Email Gateways]
    DotNetAPI -->|Media Storage| BlobStorage[Azure Blob / S3 Storage]
    DotNetAPI -->|Weather Ingestion| WeatherAPI[Weather & Mapbox APIs]
```

### Key Technical Stack Components:
- **Frontend**: React.js (Vite, Axios, Tailwind CSS/Vanilla CSS, Chart.js/Recharts).
- **Backend API**: ASP.NET Core Web API (.NET 8.0) in C#.
- **ORM / Database Access**: Entity Framework Core (EF Core) with Migrations.
- **Security / Auth**: JWT (JSON Web Tokens) with ASP.NET Core Identity (Role-based access).
- **Database**: PostgreSQL (Recommended for cost efficiency) or Microsoft SQL Server.

